Description: antinomie: keep stock structure layouts: recorder state in side tables
 A Debian guest ships Debian's own kernel modules. They load only into a
 kernel whose structures have Debian's layout: modversions compares a CRC
 of every structure an exported symbol reaches, and the kernel's BTF, which
 the guest reads, describes the same layouts. The recorder added fields to
 task_struct, io_ring_ctx, io_kiocb, aio_kiocb, io_async_msghdr and
 coredump_params; it now keeps none in a stock structure.
 .
 - Per task (cookies, the monitor and run contexts, sequence counters, the
   io_uring request a worker runs, the process core being written): a
   struct in the task's security blob, at an offset reserved after every
   LSM's own part when the LSMs are set up (security.c). No LSM is
   registered, so the LSM list, lsm_list_modules() and the boot log stay
   Debian's. The blob is allocated zeroed with the task, after
   dup_task_struct() copied the parent, and freed after the recorder's
   release, so fork no longer clears copied fields. ANTINOMIE now depends
   on SECURITY.
 - io_uring and AIO requests: the asynchronous request record is found by
   the io_kiocb or aio_kiocb it belongs to, in a table under the lock that
   already guarded the slot; while no record is alive no lock is taken. A
   request submitted again replaces whatever its object left (abandoned).
   The msghdr sizes of a recvmsg live in that record.
 - io_uring rings: the creator (tgid, task, cookies) of every ring, by ring,
   under RCU, freed with the ring.
 - Process cores: the deadline and live-acquisition state of a core the
   recorder writes are the writing task's, not coredump_params'; the ELF
   writer's helpers keep their stock prototypes.
 .
 The recorder no longer exports symbols: nothing modular uses them, and the
 export table is Debian's.
 .
 Hooked kernel files: security/security.c (the blob reservation); the
 others lose code.
Author: MikiMiku <jonathanirvinjo@gmail.com>
Origin: project
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-10-29

